# read() over a real command line. The runner gives every .cli case a pipe on
# standard input, empty unless the case has a `.in`; this one's is the file
# beside it. Inheriting ./check's own stdin instead would make this case green
# from a pipe and red from a terminal.
-e 'print(len(read()))'
-e 'print(read() |> trim |> split("\n") |> len)'

# A program that never reads never touches the pipe it is standing on. What
# that buys is that `yes | vine -e 'print(1)'` finishes; this line is only
# where it is visible at all.
-e 'print(1 + 1)'
